  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Wednesday night) \nIssued at 152 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nRich moisture will continue surging northeast into NM Monday with \ncloud decks thickening and lowering thru the day. The focus for \nheavy rainfall will be along and west of the Cont Divide Monday \nafternoon as forcing from the upper level trough strengthens over\neastern AZ and western NM. The focus then shifts eastward into \nthe central mt chain Monday night. WPC has a large 'Moderate Risk'\narea of excessive rainfall (40% chance of flash flooding within \n25km of any point). Rain rates may not be high enough to support \nsignificant flash flooding over western NM given a lower threat \nfor thunderstorms with limited instability. The more likely \nscenario is for a large area of light to moderate rain developing \nover western NM Monday to push slowly east into central NM Monday \nnight. There may be a window of opportunity for heavier rainfall \nrates within central NM Monday afternoon before the main area of \nwidespread rainfall shifts east. Antecedent soil conditions would \nbe more supportive of flash flooding within central NM compared to\nnorthwest NM where several days of drier weather have assisted \nsoil recovery.\n\nModels are still in good agreement with slowing the H5 low over AZ \nTuesday while abundant moisture from Polo surges northeast into NM. \nPWATs rise to near +3.5 standard deviations (1.2\"+) Tuesday. The \ngreater instability focuses over eastern NM where antecedent soil \nconditions are already saturated. A large 'Moderate Risk' for flash \nflooding will be in place across nearly all of NM. Rainfall rates \nare likely to be highest over eastern NM and the flash flood risk\nmay be elevated to 'High'. Meanwhile, long duration moderate rain\nwill impact northern and central NM. Area rivers will continue to\nrise with additional river flooding developing Tuesday night.\n\nThe entire system continues to trend slower into Wednesday with \nwidespread rainfall continuing over the area. The GFS and ECMWF are \nin excellent agreement with showing the H5 low near the ABQ metro \nWednesday while the remnant wave from Polo slides into eastern NM. \nWidespread moderate to locally heavy rainfall is now expected to \ncontinue thru Wednesday with antecedent soil conditions favorable \nfor another day of potential flooding. WPC has a 25% flash flood \nthreat painted over northeast NM but this may get expanded south and \nwest if model trends continue to slow. Convective instability is \nlimited again Wednesday so the event may be more widespread rain \nrather than intense rates from storms. QPF amounts are impressive\nwith another widespread 1 to 3\" expected. These amounts will \npush most areas above their record wettest September and leave \nthe official end of the monsoon season above normal (mostly thanks\nto the second half of September). The NBM 90th %tile QPF with \nsupport from several deterministic models show localized rainfall\namounts over 4\". Mid level temps are cold enough to support a few \ninches of snow in the Sangre de Cristo Mts >10,000 ft Wednesday \nnight and Thursday morning as wrap around precip continues on the \nnorth side of the departing H5 low.",
